{ Capablanca plans. Finish development with a pin: bishop to g5. [%cal Yc1g5] } 7. Bg5 { You develop your last minor piece and pin the knight to the queen. [%tail] [%cal Gc1g5] } (7. Ne2 { Also sound. Your knight clears c3 for a useful pawn advance. } 7... Ne7 { Also playable. Meanwhile your bishop still belongs on g5, pinning the knight and eyeing d8. [%cal Bc6e7] }) (7. Bxc6 { Also sound. You trade bishop for knight and change his pawn structure. } 7... bxc6 { Also playable. His structure is already worse, but your last bishop is still waiting for a square like g5. [%cal Bb7c6] }) (7. Ba4 { Also sound. You keep the bishop, but leave the other bishop at home. } 7... Bxc3 { Also playable. His bishop grabs the knight on c3, while a pin on f6 would have kept your whole army connected. [%cal Bb4c3] }) (7. h3 { A useful waiting move. It does not develop your last bishop. } 7... Ne7 { Also playable. Your last bishop is still at home on c1, while a pin on f6 would already have it in the game. [%cal Bc6e7] }) 7... Ne7 8. Bxf6 8... gxf6 9. d4 { Your pawn claims the center too. His king sits behind broken pawns on f6, f7 and h7, with the g-file wide open in front of it. [%cal Gd2d4] } 1-0


[Event "Capablanca plans: Claim room in the center"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/rnbqkbnr/ppp2ppp/3p4/4p3/4P3/5N2/PPPP1PPP/RNBQKB1R_w_KQkq_-_0_3"]
