{ His pieces face your king side. Where can your king find a safer home? } 12. O-O-O { Your king leaves the g column and your rook enters the game in one move. [%tail] [%cal Ge1c1] } (12. Rf1 { Also sound. Your rook helps defend, but your king remains in the center. } 12... O-O-O { Also playable. Your rook helps out, but your own king is still stuck in the center a move longer than castling would leave it. [%cal Be8c8] }) (12. Rg1 { Also sound. Your rook contests the g column instead of castling away. } 12... O-O-O { Also playable. Contesting the g column is useful, but it does nothing to get your own king to safety. [%cal Be8c8] }) (12. Nh4 { Also sound. Your knight asks the queen to move before you settle your king. } 12... Qg5 { Also playable. His queen has to move, but your king is still standing in the middle of the board. [%cal Bf5g5] }) (12. Qc3 { Also sound. Your queen centralizes, but it does nothing about your own king's safety. } 12... Rg8 { Also playable. His rook joins the g column, while your king is still waiting in the center. [%cal Bh8g8] }) (12. O-O { The king heads toward the side where Black has prepared his attack. } 12... Rg8 { Also playable. His rook joins the very file your king just walked into. [%cal Bh8g8] }) 12... O-O-O 13. Qe3 { Your queen centralizes, and your rook presses the d column. Both kings ended up on the same wing, so his prepared attack has nowhere to land. [%cal Gd2e3] } 1-0


[Event "Capablanca plans: Open a route for the bishop"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/r5k1/pp1q1ppp/2pp4/2b2P2/4Q3/2P5/PP1R2PP/2K1R3_b_-_-_1_24"]
