{ You gave up the exchange. Can your pawns help your bishop join the fight? } 24... d5 { You clear d6 for the bishop, so it can swing onto a live diagonal and back up your pawn majority. [%tail] [%cal Gd6d5] } (24... Qd8 { The queen steps back, but the bishop still needs a route into the game. } 25. Rde2 25... Qg5+ 26. Kc2 { This gives up some pressure. The queen retreat costs time the bishop needed to get moving. [%cal Rc1c2] }) (24... h6 { You protect the king side while the bishop remains cut off. } 25. f6 25... a5 26. fxg7 { This gives up some pressure. His pawn storms through to g7 while your own bishop is still stuck at home. [%cal Rf6g7] }) (24... h5 { The pawn gains space, but it does not free your bishop. } 25. f6 25... d5 26. fxg7 { This gives up some pressure. Your own d5 arrives a move too late here, after his f pawn already broke through. [%cal Rf6g7] }) (24... Rd8 { This gives too much away. Your rook steps aside, but it does nothing to free the bishop stuck behind your own pawns. } 25. Qe7 25... Qxe7 26. Rxe7 { This leaves you worse off. Trading queens hands him the seventh row, and your bishop never got the diagonal it needed. [%cal Re1e7] }) 25. Qf4 25... Bd6 { Your bishop swings onto the diagonal you just opened and attacks his queen on f4. [%cal Gc5d6] } 0-1


[Event "Capablanca plans: Shut the bishop out"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/r4rk1/ppp2p2/3b3p/2p1p1p1/4P3/3P1PBP/PPP2P2/R4RK1_b_-_-_0_15"]
