{ Restrict a piece. Play your pawn to f6 and keep his bishop behind its pawns. [%cal Yf7f6] } 15... f6 { Your e5 pawn stays put. His bishop on g3 has no useful path into the game. [%tail] [%cal Gf7f6] } (15... a5 { Also sound. You begin queen-side play before closing the bishop in. } 16. a4 { Also playable. Queen-side play is useful, but his bishop on g3 is not locked in yet. [%cal Ba2a4] }) (15... h5 { Also sound. You gain space, but do not secure the e5 pawn with f6 yet. } 16. h4 { Also playable. Space on the wing is nice, but e5 is still there for him to challenge without f6 first. [%cal Bh3h4] }) (15... Kh7 { Also sound. You move the king first rather than fix the pawn chain. } 16. Kh2 { Also playable. Tucking the king away is fine, but the pawn chain in front of his bishop is still open to change. [%cal Bg1h2] }) (15... Kg7 { Also sound. Your king approaches the center before you fix the pawn chain. } 16. Kg2 { Also playable. The king centralizes, but f6 is still the move that actually locks his bishop out. [%cal Bg1g2] }) (15... g4 { Your pawn advances, but it gives his bishop a diagonal. } 16. fxg4 { His f pawn takes, keeping the h pawn for an advance. } 16... Rae8 17. h4 { This leaves you worse off. Now his bishop finally has the diagonal it was missing. [%cal Rh3h4] }) 16. Kg2 16... b5 { Your pawn grabs queen-side space while his bishop stays boxed in behind its own pawns. [%cal Gb7b5] } 0-1


[Event "Capablanca plans: Occupy the hole"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/r3brk1/pp2q1pp/1np1p3/2Nn1p2/3P4/3BPN2/PP3PPP/2RQ1RK1_w_-_-_2_15"]
