{ Black has left a hole in the center. Give your knight a permanent home. } 15. Ne5 { Your knight occupies e5. Neither neighboring pawn can chase it away. [%tail] [%cal Gf3e5] } (15. Qb3 { Also strong. Your queen attacks from the flank rather than occupying e5. } 15... Rb8 { Also playable. His rook shifts to the open file, but a knight would still love e5 next move too. [%cal Ba8b8] }) (15. Qc2 { Also strong. Your queen adds central pressure while the knight waits. } 15... Nb4 { Also playable. His knight jumps to b4, though nothing there stops you from claiming e5 all the same. [%cal Bd5b4] }) (15. Qd2 { Also sound. You connect your pieces before occupying the hole. } 15... Rd8 { Also playable. His rook joins the center, and e5 is still exactly as inviting as it was. [%cal Ba8d8] }) (15. Qe1 { Also sound. Your queen shifts to e1, though the knight still wants e5 more than anything the queen move gains. } 15... Nc8 { Also playable. His knight retreats to c8, and the hole on e5 is still wide open for the taking. [%cal Bb6c8] }) (15. Nxb7 { A pawn looks free, but your knight leaves its strong central post. } 15... Qxb7 16. Qd2 16... Nd7 { This leaves you worse off. Trading the strong knight for a mere pawn gives back everything the outpost was worth. [%cal Rb6d7] }) 15... Rb8 16. Re1 16... Nd7 17. Ncxd7 { Your other knight takes the trade, and the knight on e5 stays exactly where it is — a permanent piece nothing can dislodge. [%cal Gc5d7] } 1-0


[Event "Capablanca plans: Challenge the advanced knight"]
