{ That white knight cramps your rooks. Can a pawn break give them room? } 17... c5 { You prepare d5. Attack the center instead of waiting behind the knight. [%tail] [%cal Gc6c5] } (17... d5 { Also active. You challenge the center immediately rather than prepare c5. } 18. Na4 { Also playable. Playing d5 right away skips the prep, and his knight simply hops away to a4. [%cal Bc3a4] }) (17... Kf7 { Also sound. Your king steps up, but it does nothing to challenge the center. } 18. Rf3 { Also playable. His rook lifts to the third row, while c5 and d5 still do the real work in the center. [%cal Bf1f3] }) (17... Nc8 { Also sound. Your knight retreats, but it does nothing to challenge the center. } 18. g4 { Also playable. His pawn grabs king-side space, and the c pawn is still the one that actually attacks the center. [%cal Bg2g4] }) (17... a5 { Also sound. Your pawn grabs queen-side space, but it does nothing to challenge the center. } 18. Rf3 { Also playable. His rook shifts to the third row, and c5 is still the move that opens the center. [%cal Bf1f3] }) 18. Rf3 18... d5 19. exd5 19... Bxd5 { Your bishop recaptures and comes alive on the long diagonal — it now attacks his knight on e6 too. [%cal Gb7d5] } 0-1


[Event "Capablanca plans: Use the central knight"]
