{ The queens are gone. Where can your knight attack while staying active? } 24. Nf5 { Your knight eyes d6 and h6. It joins the attack without another pawn move. [%tail] [%cal Gh4f5] } (24. b3 { Also sound. You secure the queen side before bringing the knight forward. } 24... Nd7 { Also playable. His knight comes to d7, but neither the bishop on d6 nor the pawn on h6 gets any safer. [%cal Bb8d7] }) (24. Rab1 { Also sound. Your rook gains activity while the knight stays on h4. } 24... Nd7 { Also playable. The rook shifts to b1, and a knight on f5 would still be forking the bishop and the h6 pawn. [%cal Bb8d7] }) (24. a5 { Also sound. Your pawn grabs more space, but it does nothing for the knight still waiting on h4. } 24... Nd7 { Also playable. His knight develops, though it leaves the bishop and the h6 pawn exactly as loose as before. [%cal Bb8d7] }) (24. Rfd1 { Also sound. Your rook centralizes, but it does nothing for the knight still waiting on h4. } 24... Nd7 { Also playable. His knight comes to d7, and a jump to f5 would still hit both the bishop and the h6 pawn. [%cal Bb8d7] }) (24. Bd1 { Less accurate. Your bishop retreats, but it does nothing for the knight still waiting on h4. } 24... Nd7 25. Rc1 25... Rab8 { This gives up some pressure. His rook connects, but the knight jump to f5 is still sitting there, hitting the bishop and the h6 pawn. [%cal Ra8b8] }) 24... Nd7 25. Nxh6 { Your knight scoops up the h pawn for free — every square it eyed from f5 is now paying off. [%cal Gf5h6] } 1-0


[Event "Capablanca plans: Give back the exchange"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/5qk1/pb5p/3br3/2p1p2Q/2Pp1p2/1P3P2/P3R1PP/4RNK1_w_-_-_3_29"]
