{ Now let us trade. Give back material and capture the e5 pawn with your rook. [%cal Ye2e5] } 29. Rxe5 { You return the exchange to remove the center of his attack. [%cal Ge2e5] } (29. Nd2 { Also strong. Your knight joins the defense without returning material yet. } 29... Kh8 { Also playable. His king tucks away, but the exchange sac hasn't happened yet to open the position up. [%cal Bg8h8] }) (29. Kh1 { Less accurate. Your king tucks into the corner, but the center still needs clearing. } 29... Qf7 30. Qh3 30... Kg7 { This gives up some pressure. Giving back the exchange on e5 was still the move that clears the center. [%cal Rg8g7] }) (29. Qh3 { Less accurate. Your queen shifts to h3, but the center still needs clearing. } 29... Bc8 30. Qh4 30... Bc7 { This gives up some pressure. His bishop shuffles home, and the exchange sac was still the cleaner way to clear the center. [%cal Rd6c7] }) (29. Kf2 { This gives too much away. Your king wanders toward the center instead of addressing his attack. } 29... Rh6 30. Qg4+ 30... Rg6 { This leaves you worse off. Check drives the rook back, and returning the exchange was still the safer path. [%cal Rh6g6] }) 29... Bxe5 { His bishop takes. Use your other rook to take the bishop on e5. [%cal Ye1e5] } 30. Rxe5 { The dangerous bishop and central pawn are gone. Your rook is active. [%cal Ge1e5] } (30. Ng3 { This gives too much away. Your knight sidesteps instead of recapturing the bishop on e5. } 30... Qf6 31. Ne4 31... Bxe4 { This leaves you worse off. His bishop grabs the knight, and recapturing on e5 right away was the safer path. [%cal Rb7e4] }) (30. Nd2 { This gives too much away. Your knight retreats instead of recapturing the bishop on e5. } 30... Qf6 31. Kf1 31... Bc6 { This leaves you worse off. His bishop settles safely on c6, while recapturing on e5 kept the attack contained. [%cal Rb7c6] }) (30. a4 { This gives too much away. Your pawn advances on the wing instead of recapturing the bishop on e5. } 30... Qe7 31. Qg4+ 31... Kh8 { This leaves you worse off. Check chases the king to the corner, and recapturing on e5 right away avoided all of this. [%cal Rg8h8] }) (30. Qg5+ { This gives too much away. The check gains a tempo, but the bishop on e5 is still hanging there. } 30... Qg7 31. Qf5 31... Re7 { This leaves you worse off. His rook re-routes to safety, and recapturing on e5 immediately kept more pressure on. [%cal Re6e7] }) 1-0


[Event "Capablanca plans: Exchange the important defender"]
