{ His bishop guards the light squares near his king. Can you challenge it? } 19. Bf3 { Your bishop offers a trade. His king will miss that light-squared guard. [%cal Ge2f3] } (19. c5 { Also strong. Your pawn attacks the king position before exchanging bishops. } 19... f5 { Also playable. His pawn steps up, and the light-squared bishop trade is still waiting to happen. [%cal Bf6f5] }) (19. a4 { Also sound. Your pawn expands on the queen side, but it leaves his light-squared guard alone. } 19... Qf8 { Also playable. His queen tucks back, and the bishop trade near his king still hasn't happened. [%cal Bg7f8] }) (19. Nf3 { Less accurate. This knight blocks your own bishop's path to f3. } 19... Rde8 20. c5 20... e5 { This gives up some pressure. His pawn breaks in the center, and the bishop trade near his king was still the more testing plan. [%cal Re6e5] }) (19. Bh5 { Less accurate. This detour doesn't trade the light-squared bishops, which was the real point. } 19... Ba6 20. Bf3 20... f5 { This gives up some pressure. He gets an extra developing move in, and the direct trade would have avoided that entirely. [%cal Rf6f5] }) 19... Rge8 { His rook move is a mistake. Can you finish the useful exchange? } 20. Bxb7 { His light-squared defender is gone. Your c pawn can help open the attack. [%cal Gf3b7] } (20. c5 { Also sound. Your pawn claims space, but it leaves the exchange on b7 sitting there unplayed. } 20... c6 { Also playable. His pawn blocks the diagonal, and the exchange on b7 is off the table for now. [%cal Bc7c6] }) (20. a4 { Also sound. Your pawn continues its advance, but the exchange on b7 is still unplayed. } 20... Qf8 { Also playable. His queen shuffles back, and the exchange on b7 is still there for you to take. [%cal Bg7f8] }) (20. Ra3 { Also sound. Your rook swings to a3, but it leaves the exchange on b7 sitting there unplayed. } 20... c5 { Also playable. His pawn advances, and the exchange on b7 is still yours whenever you want it. [%cal Bc7c5] }) (20. Rb3 { Less accurate. This rook square does nothing to remove the bishop guarding his king. } 20... Qf8 21. c5 21... e5 { This gives up some pressure. His center pawn advances, and removing that defender right away was still the cleaner plan. [%cal Re6e5] }) 1-0


[Event "Capablanca plans: Trade queens without losing activity"]
