{ The queens can come off. Keep your knights working together. } 18. Nd4 { You centralize first, leaving the other knight ready to take the queen. [%cal Gf3d4] } (18. a3 { Also sound. Your pawn secures b4, but it does nothing to prepare the queen trade. } 18... h6 { Also playable. His pawn makes luft, and your knights still haven't sorted out the coming trade. [%cal Bh7h6] }) (18. Nd2 { Also sound. This knight heads toward the center from a different angle. } 18... Qxe2 { Also playable. He trades queens right away, and your knights are not yet arranged to recapture cleanly. [%cal Bc4e2] }) (18. Ne5 { Also sound. This knight jumps to an outpost instead of preparing the queen trade. } 18... Qc7 { Also playable. His queen retreats, and your knights still haven't settled how they'll recapture. [%cal Bc4c7] }) (18. Qd2 { Also sound. Offering the trade yourself is fine, but it doesn't sort out which knight recaptures. } 18... b4 { Also playable. His pawn pushes on, and the knights still need to agree on e2. [%cal Bb5b4] }) (18. Rd4 { The rook attacks the queen, but it changes how your pieces can recapture. } 18... Qxe2 { Also playable. He trades queens anyway, and the rook on d4 is not what keeps your knights connected. [%cal Bc4e2] }) 18... Qxe2 { He trades queens too soon. Which knight keeps your pieces connected? } 19. Ncxe2 { One knight holds d4 and the other supports it. Your pieces stay connected. [%cal Gc3e2] } (19. Ndxe2 { Less accurate. This knight abandons d4, the square the pair was working to hold. } 19... h6 20. Bh4 20... b4 { This gives up some pressure. His pawn advances, and recapturing with the other knight would have kept d4 covered. [%cal Rb5b4] }) (19. Nf5 { This gives too much away. This knight jumps forward and ignores the queen hanging on e2. } 19... Qxd1+ 20. Nxd1 20... Re8 { This leaves you worse off. His rook develops freely, and recapturing on e2 first was the move that kept everything connected. [%cal Rf8e8] }) (19. e4 { This gives too much away. This pawn push ignores the queen hanging on e2. } 19... Qg4 20. Nf5 20... Qxg5 { His queen takes your bishop, not just the e pawn. Your attack has cost a piece. [%cal Rg4g5] }) (19. Bxf6 { This gives too much away. This trade ignores the queen hanging on e2. } 19... Qxd1+ 20. Nxd1 20... Bxf6 { This leaves you worse off. He recaptures the bishop, and recapturing on e2 first was the move that kept your knights connected. [%cal Re7f6] }) 1-0


[Event "Capablanca plans: Break against the fixed pawn"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/4r1k1/pp1bqnpp/2p1pr2/4Np2/2PP4/3B3Q/PP2RPPP/4R1K1_w_-_-_1_19"]
