{ A central pawn break. Push to d5 and attack the pawn holding his pieces back. [%cal Yd4d5] } 19. d5 { You challenge e6 while your rooks and bishop are ready to use the open lines. [%cal Gd4d5] } (19. c5 { Also strong. You gain space and restrict the bishop instead of opening the center. } 19... Rh6 { Also playable. His rook shifts to h6, and the e6 pawn is still sitting there unchallenged. [%cal Bf6h6] }) (19. Nxf7 { Also strong. You exchange the central knight rather than break with the pawn. } 19... Rxf7 { His rook takes, keeping queen and king in place. This trade is also playable. [%cal Bf6f7] }) (19. Rd2 { Also sound. Your rook shifts to d2, but the center pawn break is still unplayed. } 19... Rd8 { Also playable. His rook centralizes, and the e6 pawn is still fixed and waiting. [%cal Be8d8] }) (19. Qe3 { Also sound. Your queen centralizes, but the center pawn break is still unplayed. } 19... Rd8 { Also playable. His rook meets you in the center, and e6 remains a fixed target. [%cal Be8d8] }) (19. Qg3 { Also sound. Your queen eyes the king side, but the center pawn break is still unplayed. } 19... Bc8 { Also playable. His bishop retreats home, and the e6 pawn is still fixed in place. [%cal Bd7c8] }) (19. Nxd7 { You remove the bishop, but your own knight leaves its powerful post. } 19... Qxd7 20. Qe3 20... f4 { This leaves you worse off. His pawn advances instead, and giving up the strong knight was not worth it. [%cal Rf5f4] }) 19... Nxe5 { Taking d5 invites your bishop to c4. He trades knights. Use your rook on e5. [%cal Ye2e5] } 20. Rxe5 { Your rook stays active on e5. His e6 pawn still ties down his pieces. [%cal Ge2e5] } (20. d6 { This gives too much away. Pushing the pawn lets his queen take it back for free. } 20... Qxd6 21. Rxe5 21... c5 { This leaves you worse off. His pawn advances on the queen side, and recapturing on e5 right away kept more control. [%cal Rc6c5] }) (20. dxe6 { This gives too much away. This capture hands over the e file for nothing extra. } 20... Rxe6 21. Bxf5 21... Rh6 { This leaves you worse off. His rook swings to h6, and keeping the rook active on e5 was the steadier plan. [%cal Re6h6] }) (20. Bc2 { This gives too much away. Your bishop retreats instead of recapturing on e5. } 20... Ng6 21. dxe6 21... Nf4 { This leaves you worse off. His knight jumps to f4, and recapturing on e5 immediately avoided that entirely. [%cal Rg6f4] }) (20. Bb1 { This gives too much away. Your bishop tucks into the corner instead of recapturing on e5. } 20... Ng6 21. dxe6 21... Nf4 { This leaves you worse off. His knight lands on f4 again, and the rook recapture on e5 was still the tidier answer. [%cal Rg6f4] }) 1-0


[Event "Capablanca plans: Mobilize the king-side majority"]
