{ You have more king-side pawns. Can you start putting that majority to work? } 30. h4 { Your h pawn takes space and starts the king-side majority marching forward. [%tail] [%cal Gh2h4] } (30. g3 { Also winning. Your g pawn prepares the majority in a different order. } 30... Ne6 { Also playable. Starting with g3 works too, and the h pawn still wants its turn to advance. [%cal Bf4e6] }) (30. b3 { Also sound. Your pawn secures the queen side, but it does nothing for the majority on the other wing. } 30... Bc5 { Also playable. His bishop repositions, while the real business is still three pawns against one on the king side. [%cal Bd6c5] }) (30. Rac1 { Also sound. Your rook centralizes, but it does nothing for the majority on the other wing. } 30... Bc5 { Also playable. His bishop shifts to c5, and the king-side pawns still haven't started rolling. [%cal Bd6c5] }) (30. Kf1 { Also sound. Your king heads toward the center, but it does nothing for the majority on the other wing. } 30... a5 { Also playable. His pawn advances on the queen side, while your own majority is still waiting on h4 alone. [%cal Ba6a5] }) 30... Ne6 31. g3 { Your pawn joins the push: three pawns now face his lone f pawn, and the h pawn already has no enemy pawn left on its own file or the file next door. [%cal Gg2g3] } 1-0


