{ His king must help defend the pawns. Can you give your queen a way in? } 24. h4 { Your h pawn challenges g5. A capture opens the h column for your queen. [%tail] [%cal Gh3h4] } (24. c5 { Less accurate. Your pawn grabs queen-side space, but his king still needs to worry about the other wing. } 24... g6 25. h4 25... gxh4 { This gives up some pressure. The h pawn break still arrives a move later, just as strong. [%cal Rg5h4] }) (24. Qg4 { The queen moves first, but the pawn break can open lines more directly. } 24... Ne6 25. h4 25... gxh4 { This gives up some pressure. Moving the queen first only delays the same h pawn break. [%cal Rg5h4] }) (24. a3 { Less accurate. Your pawn makes room on the queen side, but his king still needs to worry about the other wing. } 24... g6 25. h4 25... gxh4 { This gives up some pressure. The h pawn still gets there and opens the same file. [%cal Rg5h4] }) (24. Qh7 { Less accurate. Your queen steps forward alone, without the pawn break to open a road for her. } 24... Ne6 25. h4 25... g4 { This gives up some pressure. He avoids the capture this time, but the h pawn still gains the space it wanted. [%cal Rg5g4] }) 24... gxh4 25. Qxh4+ { Your queen recaptures on the newly opened h column, and it comes with check — his king has nowhere safe to hide. [%cal Ge4h4] } 1-0


[Event "Capablanca plans: Stop the freeing pawn advance"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/4r1k1/pb3ppp/1pN5/4P3/8/2R5/PP1r1PPP/1R4K1_b_-_-_0_22"]
