{ White would like to play f4 and free his pieces. Can a pawn stop it? } 22... g5 { Your g pawn takes f4 away. The pressure on e5 keeps his knight tied down. [%tail] [%cal Gg7g5] } (22... g6 { Also sound. You support the pawn chain instead of advancing it at once. } 23. a3 { Also playable. Support first is fine, but g5 is the move that actually takes f4 away from him. [%cal Ba2a3] }) (22... Bxc6 { Also sound. Your bishop trades off the knight, but it does nothing about his freeing break. } 23. Rxc6 { Also playable. His rook recaptures, and f4 is still available to him without the g pawn in the way. [%cal Bc3c6] }) (22... h6 { Also sound. Your pawn shores up the king side, but it does nothing about his freeing break. } 23. a3 { Also playable. He gains a tempo on the queen side, while f4 is still open to him. [%cal Ba2a3] }) (22... h5 { Also sound. Your pawn grabs space on the wing, but it does nothing about his freeing break. } 23. a4 { Also playable. His pawn answers on the other wing, and f4 remains a move he can still make. [%cal Ba2a4] }) (22... Rxe5 { That pawn is defended by the knight. Taking it loses a rook. } 23. Nxe5 23... g6 24. Nf3 { This leaves you worse off. Giving up the rook for a pawn was never the way to stop f4 — g5 does that for free. [%cal Re5f3] }) 23. h3 23... Bxc6 24. Rxc6 24... Rxe5 { Your rook collects the e5 pawn now that nothing defends it — the pressure you kept up finally cashes in. [%cal Ge8e5] } 0-1


