{ A rook lift. Move your rook to d3 so the other rook can join it on d1. [%cal Yd1d3] } 15. Rd3 { Your rook leaves room on d1. The pair can challenge the center together. [%cal Gd1d3] } (15. Kb2 { Also sound. Your king tucks away, but the rook still hasn't found its lift to d3. } 15... c5 { Also playable. His pawn advances, and the rook lift to d3 still hasn't happened. [%cal Bc7c5] }) (15. a3 { Also sound. Your pawn makes luft, but the rook still hasn't found its lift to d3. } 15... c5 { Also playable. His pawn matches yours, and the rook lift is still waiting to happen. [%cal Bc7c5] }) (15. Qb3 { Less accurate. Your queen leaves the defense of d4 a move too soon. } 15... e5 16. Rhe1 16... exd4 { This gives up some pressure. His pawn grabs the center, and the rook lift would have kept d4 defended. [%cal Re5d4] }) (15. Kb1 { Less accurate. This king move delays the rook lift by a tempo. } 15... f5 16. Rd3 16... Nf6 { This gives up some pressure. His knight develops, and the lift arrives a move later than it needed to. [%cal Rd7f6] }) (15. Nh4 { This gives too much away. Your knight chooses a different task. } 15... Bxh1 { His bishop takes a rook, not just a pawn. Your knight left the diagonal open. } 16. Rxh1 16... Qxa2 { This leaves you worse off. His queen grabs a second pawn, and the rook lift never even got the chance to matter. [%cal Ra5a2] }) 15... Kb8 { He steps back too soon. That is a mistake. Bring your h rook to d1. [%cal Yh1d1] } 16. Rhd1 { Both rooks face the center. Your lift made room without blocking the queen. [%cal Gh1d1] } (16. Ra3 { Also sound. The rook changes fronts instead of doubling on the d column. } 16... Qb4 { Also playable. His queen jumps forward, and the second rook still hasn't reached the center. [%cal Ba5b4] }) (16. Re1 { Also sound. This rook heads to e1 instead of doubling with its partner. } 16... e5 { Also playable. His pawn claims the center, and the second rook is still one square from d1. [%cal Be6e5] }) (16. a3 { Less accurate. This pawn move does nothing to bring the second rook into play. } 16... e5 17. dxe5 17... Nc5 { This gives up some pressure. His knight lands on a good square, and doubling the rooks first would have kept more control. [%cal Rd7c5] }) (16. Kb2 { Less accurate. This king move does nothing to bring the second rook into play either. } 16... e5 17. Rhd1 17... e4 { This gives up some pressure. His pawn pushes on, and the rooks were already doubled without this detour. [%cal Re5e4] }) 1-0


[Event "Capablanca plans: Transfer the rook across"]
