{ Your first rook is already active. Find a route across for the second one. } 24... Re6 { The sixth rank is your bridge to g6. Both rooks can attack the king side. [%tail] [%cal Ge8e6] } (24... Rxb2 { Also sound. You collect a pawn, but the second rook has not joined the attack. } 25. Nd4 { His knight stays central instead of taking your a pawn. This is also playable. [%cal Bc6d4] }) (24... a5 { Also sound. Your pawn grabs queen-side space, but the second rook is still on e8, not yet on its way. } 25. b4 { Also playable. His pawn answers on the queen side, and the rook still needs the sixth rank to get across. [%cal Bb2b4] }) (24... a6 { Also sound. Your pawn makes luft on the queen side, but the second rook is still on e8, not yet on its way. } 25. Re4 { Also playable. His rook centralizes, while your own second rook is still waiting for the sixth rank. [%cal Be1e4] }) (24... h3 { Also sound. Your pawn lunges forward on the king side, but the second rook is still on e8, not yet on its way. } 25. gxh3 { Also playable. He grabs the pawn, and the second rook still needs the sixth rank to join in. [%cal Bg2h3] }) 25. Rec1 25... Kg7 26. b4 26... b5 27. Kf1 27... Rg6 { Your rook completes the bridge, landing on g6 — both rooks now bear down on his king side. [%cal Ge6g6] } 0-1


