{ His rook has taken a pawn. Can your own rook create a stronger threat? } 38... Rb8 { Your rook takes the open b column. His king, not the lost pawn, is the target. [%tail] [%cal Gd8b8] } (38... Re8 { This gives too much away. Your rook stays passive on the e column instead of grabbing the open one. } 39. h4 39... Bxh4 40. Rd7 { His rook reaches d7. The direct b-column attack would have kept him defending. [%cal Rd5d7] }) (38... Rc8 { This gives too much away. Your rook doubles up on the c column instead of the open b column. } 39. h4 39... Rb8 { Your rook targets the king instead of collecting the h pawn. } 40. R1d2 { White gets time to coordinate. Going straight to b8 would keep the initiative. [%cal Rd1d2] }) (38... Qc6 { This gives too much away. Your queen retreats to guard c6, giving up the initiative completely. } 39. g5 39... Bxg5 40. f6 { His f pawn keeps advancing. The direct rook invasion was more forcing. [%cal Rf5f6] }) (38... Rf8 { This gives too much away. Your rook heads toward f8, away from the column that actually presses his king. } 39. g5 39... hxg5 40. Qc2 { White has time to organize. Your rook belonged on the attacking b column. [%cal Rg2c2] }) (38... Qxd1+ { You give up your queen for both rooks, but your attack loses force. } 39. Rxd1 39... Rxd1+ 40. Kc2 { Your attack loses force. Keeping the queen makes the b-column attack stronger. [%cal Rb1c2] }) 39. R1d2 39... c4 { Your pawn advances again. Queen and rook both bear down on his king's wing — the pawn he grabbed on d5 is the least of your worries now. [%cal Gc5c4] } 0-1


